// spec: TODO.md §19 — variant resolution by scrutinee: no Option. prefix needed
// spec: syntax.md §7 — a variant name in an arm resolves against the matched enum
// out: some
// out: none
// PENDING the T3.9 scrutinee-resolution law (TODO.md §19, ratified but
// not implemented): the bare `None` arm must resolve against the
// scrutinee's enum — no `Option.` prefix. Today the bare name parses
// as an ordinary binder pattern, so the match fails exhaustiveness
// ("missing Option.None") — that check failure is the pending marker.
// The tuple-payload half of the law (`Some(v) => …` bare) cannot live
// here yet: `Some(` does not even parse today (arm grammar rejects the
// paren). When T3.9 lands this case goes green and can grow that arm.
fn describe(o: ?i32) -> string {
  return match o {
    Option.Some(v) => "some",
    None => "none",
  };
}

fn main() -> i32 {
  printf("{}\n", describe(Option.Some(3)));
  printf("{}\n", describe(Option.None));
  return 0;
}
